  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Months of fatigue, low mood, reduced appetite, and unintended weight loss with intermittent shortness of breath/palpitations (notably in contained spaces like lifts); headaches and nausea reported as well.\n- Symptoms: Low energy and depressed mood for months; decreased appetite with gradual weight loss (~1 stone over ~6 months); intermittent nausea; occasional headaches; shortness of breath specifically when climbing into lifts or in confined spaces; ability to walk about a mile without dyspnea; no persistent cough; no diarrhoea; no per-rectal blood; no vomiting.\n- Onset/duration: Mood and energy changes described as long-standing; appetite loss ongoing; weight loss began before the ~3.5 months ago change in job; last ~6 months for weight loss; SOB symptoms observed in the context of palpitations (lifting stairs/elevator) but not on typical exertion.\n- Past medical history: Known prior hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid) treated with thyroxine in the past; currently not taking thyroxine since changing doctors; no recent blood tests for thyroid or other labs.\n- Medications/allergies: Previously on thyroxine (thyroid replacement); not taking it presently; no listed drug allergies.\n- Family/social history: Wife has complained about nighttime/“nightly activities”; employed as a lawyer; currently in a new law firm; no other drug use or smoking information provided.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: No vital signs or physical examination findings documented in transcript; physician indicated no urgent physical exam needed at that moment.\n- Labs/imaging (if mentioned): Plan to obtain a general blood panel (hemogram for anemia, renal function, liver function, vitamins, etc.); no labs or imaging results provided in transcript.\n- Summary impression: A constellation of fatigue, weight loss, poor appetite, mood changes, and episodic palpitations with dyspnea warrants evaluation for endocrine, psychiatric, hematologic, and infectious/inflammatory processes, with particular attention to thyroid status given recent non-adherence to thyroid replacement therapy.\n- Next steps discussed: Arrange general blood tests; if results normal, proceed to physical examination; if symptoms worsen (Chest pain or new bowel changes), contact care team; provide support contact information and follow-up scheduling via app/SMS.\n",
